sec.p:
All‐inorganic lead halide perovskites have received tremendous interest owing to their potential uses in high‐performance optoelectronic devices, such as light‐emitting diodes (LEDs), solar cells, X‐ray detectors, and lasers.[
1 , 2 , 3 , 4
] One of the main reasons for the high performance is their high defect tolerance compared with classical semiconductors, such as Si, GaAs, CdTe, and InP, which is caused by a low density of intrinsic defect states due to the ionic nature and benign electronic structure of halide perovskites. Compared to polycrystalline microcrystals, colloidal halide perovskite nanocrystals (NCs) offer the unique feature of good colloidal dispersivity in nonpolar solvents along with exhibiting large exciton binding energy, thus making them promising for fabricating quantum light sources, forming superlattices, and investigating light‐ matter interaction.[
5
] Yet, structural imperfections in perovskite NCs generally lead to the formation of shallow trap levels with low absorption cross‐sections, which may result in the weakening of their photoluminescence (PL).[
6 , 7 , 8
] On the other hand, despite significant progress achieved on lead halide perovskite NCs, the toxicity of Pb hampers their use in real‐world devices and simultaneously drives the development of stable lead‐free halide perovskites.[
9 , 10 , 11 , 12
] Beyond the direct replacement of Pb (II) with Sn (II) or Ge (II),[
13 , 14
] two Pb2+ cations can be substituted with a pair of a monovalent cation (BI) and one trivalent cation (BIII), leading to the formation of the so‐called double perovskite structure with the general formula A2BIBIIIX6.[
12 , 15 , 16
] On the other hand, the replacement of two Pb2+ cations with a combination of a quadrivalent cation (BIV) and a vacancy results in the formation of the so‐called “vacancy‐ordered double perovskites” with the general formula A2BIVX6.[
17 , 18 , 19
] In contrast to lead halide perovskite NCs, lead‐free double perovskite NCs generally exhibit weak and broad emission due to the strong electron‐phonon coupling, deep‐level defects, or indirect energy band structure.[
20
] Furthermore, the broad emission of A2BIVX6 perovskites is generally attributed to the formation of self‐trapped excitons (STE).[
21
] This has been a major issue for lead‐free perovskite materials, and thus obtaining those with higher PL quantum yield (PL QY) is an important area of research efforts.

To achieve high PL QY, an additional radiative channel can be created in double perovskites via the introduction of dopants such as Sb3+, Bi3+, Te4+, and La3+ (La: lanthanide) ions.[
18 , 22 , 23
] In this regard, bulk crystals of Cs2ZrCl6 perovskites doped with Te4+ and Sb3+ ions have received significant interest, as they exhibited PL QY as high as 96%.[
23 , 24
] Despite such near‐unity PL QY of doped bulk perovskites, the corresponding doped Cs2BIVCl6 NC counterparts exhibit relatively weak PL intensities. In particular, the decrease of their size into the nanometre ranges negatively influences both the host (double perovskite) and dopant ion emissions (Figure S1, Supporting Information). Tang et al. reported that Sb‐doped Cs2SnCl6 bulk crystals exhibited a relatively high PL QY of 37%,[
25
] compared to only 8.25% for the NCs of similar chemical composition.[
26
] This trend was also observed for other doped lead‐free double perovskites.[
27
] One of the possible explanations for their low PL QY is the sub‐bandgap exciton trapping due to surface defects shortly after excitation (∼50–100 ps), as derived from transient absorption studies.[
28 , 29
] This hypothesis needs comprehensive spectroscopic and theoretical studies. Meanwhile, the chemical origin of deep‐trap defects in Cs2BIVCl6 NCs has not been investigated yet, and the relative shift of defect levels compared to singlet/triplet levels of host and dopants is not fully explained between Cs2BIVCl6 nano‐ and bulk crystals. Therefore, understanding the origin of low PL QY and obtaining highly emissive Cs2BIVCl6 NCs are challenging but important for the design of luminescent lead‐free metal halides.

In this report, to study the origin of the lower PL QY of Cs2BIVCl6 NCs as compared to their bulk counterparts, we synthesized colloidal Cs2ZrCl6:Te4+ NCs using a modified hot injection approach. To identify the difference in carrier dynamics of Cs2ZrCl6:Te4+ NCs and bulk powders, we utilized temperature‐dependent spectroscopic technologies, as these methods are widely recognized for studying exciton‐phonon coupling and non‐radiative relaxation processes in semiconductors.[
30 , 31 , 32
] We found that more pronounced lattice distortions in Cs2ZrCl6:Te4+ NCs decrease the activation energy of energy transfer and further lead to stronger non‐radiative relaxation. This is reflected not only in the decrease of the PL QY of the NCs but also in the abnormal increase of their PL lifetimes at T > 130 K. Thus, the PL decays of Cs2ZrCl6:Te4+ NCs, compared to those of bulk powder samples, exhibited an additional component. This phenomenon can also be triggered by the weak confinement regime in Cs2ZrCl6:Te4+ NCs by lowering the STE energy level.[
33
] To further support our assumptions, we have optimized the synthetic procedure to obtain Cs2ZrCl6:Te4+ NCs with gradually increasing diameters up to 40 nm, and for the largest size, the PL QY was boosted to 24%, as compared to 7% for the 22 nm NCs. In addition, efficient near‐infrared (NIR) emission was achieved for Cs2ZrCl6:Te4+ NCs by sensitization from triplet states of Te4+ to Ln3+ cations (Ln = Yb3+, Er3+, and Nd3+) via co‐doping with the latter. Our study explored the optical properties of vacancy‐ordered double perovskites in the weak confinement regime and thus built up a foundation for further improvements in their emission performance.

The bulk crystals of Te4+‐doped Cs2ZrCl6 were prepared using a procedure described previously.[

23

] The morphology of the Cs2ZrCl6:Te4+ crystals in the powder was revealed by scanning electron microscopy (SEM), as shown in Figure S2 (Supporting Information). The powder is composed of irregular columnar particles with an average long axis of 2.5±1.0 µm and a short axis of 1.7±0.7 µm (Figure S3, Supporting Information). On the other hand, the NCs of the same chemical composition have been rarely reported. Therefore, to obtain Cs2ZrCl6:Te4+ NCs, a standard hot injection approach was modified as illustrated in Figure

1a. In the classic organometallic synthesis of the lead halide perovskite NCs, metal halide (PbX2), oleic acid (OA), and oleylamine (OlAm) are mixed in octadecene (ODE) solution and form an intermediate complex, which then reacts quickly with Cs oleate. Directly adopting this approach, we first mixed ZrCl4, TeCl4, and a quaternary ammonium salt (didodecyldimethylammonium chloride, DDAC), followed by the addition of ODE, OA, and Olam into the reaction mixture. The flask was heated up until the temperature of 180 °C was reached and stabilized, in open air. However, we found that the presence of organic ligands (OA and Olam) led to the reduction of Te4+ to Te0 with an observable formation of a black precipitate in the solution (those black particles are highlighted within a white circle in Figure S4a, Supporting Information). Next, we have confirmed that the chemical environment during this synthesis is essential. ZrCl4, TeCl4, and DDAC powders were mixed with ODE in a flask, while excluding the organic ligands. The flask was degassed for 1 h at 100 °C and then switched to N2 atmosphere. Under these conditions, the Te4+ precursor would turn to black precipitate during the heating, indicating that the presence of O2 in open air is beneficial to protect Te4+ cations at high temperature (Figure S4b, Supporting Information). Interestingly, conducting the same reaction in open air (without any prior degassing steps) and replacing ODE with mesitylene also avoided the formation of Te0 in solution. This means that the OlAm ligand should be excluded from this reaction to avoid the presence of any trace of metallic tellurium (Te0). It was previously reported that multiple electron transfer paths (Te6+/Te4+/Te2+/Te0/Te−2) do exist when benzyltriethylammonium tellurium iodide was applied as a cathode in zinc ion batteries.[

34

] X‐ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) also revealed spontaneous Te0 to Te4+ conversion when exposed to open air, indicating a strong oxidation effect of O2 on Te0.[

35

] These data, alongside our observations, prove that the valence of Te is sensitive to chemical redox reactions. Furthermore, a small amount of concentrated HCl aqueous solution was added to increase the solubility of ZrCl4 and TeCl4 (see Experimental section for details). The obtained Cs2ZrCl6:Te4+ NCs were purified before the following characterisations.

Transmission electron microscopy (TEM) images show that the produced Cs2ZrCl6:Te4+ NCs have a quasi‐spherical shape with an average diameter of 22 ± 2.5 nm (Figure 1b). Figure S5a (Supporting Information) provides a high‐resolution TEM (HRTEM) image of a single Cs2ZrCl6:Te4+ NC, which exhibits quasi‐spherical morphology and clear lattice fringes. The corresponding Fast Fourier Transforms of such HRTEM images frequently reveal an interplanar distance of 0.367 nm, in coincidence with (220) lattice plane of cubic‐phase Cs2ZrCl6 (Figure S5b, Supporting Information). To validate that the obtained NCs preserve the same crystal structure as the Cs2ZrCl6:Te4+ bulk powders, X‐ray diffraction (XRD) patterns were collected (Figure 1c). The XRD patterns for both nano‐ and bulk crystals match well with the standard Cs2ZrCl6 reference pattern (PDF# 74–0505); they belong to the cubic Fm3 m structure with a lattice parameter of 1.041 nm. Bulk powders of Cs2ZrCl6:Te4+ exhibit a slight shift (≈0.1 deg) to lower angles as compared to the Cs2ZrCl6 standard, which is due to the incorporation of Te4+ dopant whose ionic radius is larger than that of Zr4+ (97 pm versus 70 pm, respectively). XRD peaks of Cs2ZrCl6:Te4+ NCs are much broader, while their maxima have the same positions as the standard Cs2ZrCl6 file (Figure S6a, Supporting Information). The possible explanation is that lattice expansion due to Te4+ doping in NCs compensates the non‐stoichiometric composition with an overall preservation of the interplanar distances similar to those in undoped crystals. In addition, lattice displacement can also result in broadening of the XRD peaks; however, the largest influence on this parameter should originate from the size effect, in accordance with the Scherrer equation. Based on the XRD data, the average size of the synthesized Cs2ZrCl6:Te4+ NCs is estimated as 25 nm, which is slightly larger than the mean size estimated from TEM images (22 nm). XPS was used to estimate the concentration of Te4+ in the doped Cs2ZrCl6:Te4+ NCs (Figure S6b and Table S3, Supporting Information).[

36

] For this purpose, we utilized strong XPS peaks centered ≈580 eV (corresponding to the Te 3d 3/2 and 3d 5/2 orbitals), resulting in a calculated Te/Zr ratio of 0.1.

The optical properties of Cs2ZrCl6:Te4+ nano‐ and bulk crystals were first studied by steady‐state absorption and PL measurements. As shown in Figure 1d (upper panel), the absorption spectrum of the bulk powder includes three main bands: below 250 nm, 300 to 360 nm, and 360 to 450 nm; these bands correspond to the absorption of the Cs2ZrCl6 host and the 1S0→3P2 and 1S0→3P1 transitions of Te4+ dopant, respectively.[

37

] The broad absorption band with two maxima at ≈400 nm is caused by the Jahn‐Teller splitting of the 3P1,0 state in Te4+.[

38

] Similar bands can also be recognized in the absorption spectrum of NCs (Figure 1d, bottom panel), albeit with a larger Jahn‐Teller splitting of the 3P1,0 state. Both Cs2ZrCl6:Te4+ NCs and bulk powders feature a broad PL band with a maximum at 580 nm that originates from the Te4+ dopant when excited at 395 nm (Figure 1d), but with very different PL QY. When excited at 395 nm, the absolute PL QY of Cs2ZrCl6:Te4+ NCs (with an average size of 22 nm) is ≈7%, while it reaches 65% for the corresponding bulk crystals with similar Te4+ concentration. It is important to note that this is not the possible maximum PL QY of Cs2ZrCl6:Te4+ bulk powders (over 95%) because this value is significantly influenced by the Te/Zr ratio, as indicated in our previous report.[

23

] In this work, to ensure a valid comparison, we intentionally selected bulk powders with a similar chemical composition but lower PL QY, as it was shown before.[

23

] PL excitation (PLE) spectrum in Figure 1d shows that luminescence from Te4+ can be excited via two pathways: (i) directly through S0→3P1,0 transition in Te4+ (e.g., at 395 nm), and (ii) through the energy transfer from the host (excitation at < 260 nm). Noticeably, for the NCs, the PLE intensity at 300 nm is much higher than at 400 nm, in contrast to bulk powders with similar values at the mentioned wavelengths, which is revealed by the solid fill area in Figure 1d. This offers a hint that the mechanisms of energy transfer from the host to the dopant are different in the case of the bulk and the NC forms of Cs2ZrCl6:Te4+. These observations prompted us to examine the exciton‐phonon coupling and PL evolution in Cs2ZrCl6:Te4+ NCs and bulk powders using temperature‐dependent PL spectroscopy.

The changes in the intensity and shape of the PL spectra of Cs2ZrCl6:Te4+ NCs and bulk powders with temperature (changing from cryogenic to room temperature) are shown in Figure
2a,b. To explore the pathway of the intermediate defects from the Cs2ZrCl6 host to Te4+ dopant, the PL intensity of both the host (at 450 nm) and dopant (at 580 nm) bands was analysed upon excitation at 250 nm. For the proper analysis, all the PL spectra initially measured in nm units (Figures S7, Supporting Information) were subjected to the Jacobian transformation into the units of eV (Figures S8, Supporting Information).[
39
] As shown in Figure 2c, the probability of radiative recombination via the Te4+ transition is the largest at cryogenic temperatures, which is signified by the highest intensity of the dopant PL band. The intensity of the signal is preserved until the temperature reaches 130 and 170 K for NCs and bulk powders, respectively. Upon further temperature increase, the PL intensity of the dopant in both NCs and bulk crystals decreases by a factor of approximately two; however, the dopant emission in NCs decreases faster. It is worth mentioning that the described trends for Te4+ are well reproduced with direct excitation of the Te4+ band at 395 nm (Figure S9, Supporting Information), which also demonstrates that the presence of ligands on the surface does not affect the optical properties of NCs. On the contrary, a reverse trend is observed for the host emission in both NCs and bulk powders. The emission intensity of the host increases in the temperature range of 50–170 K and then remains unchanged even at room temperatures. However, the increase of the PL intensity at 450 nm is almost two times higher for NCs as compared to the bulk sample (Figure 2c, lower panel).[
40 , 41
] In other words, for T >150 K, the energy transfer from the Cs2ZrCl6 host to the Te4+ dopant is more effective in bulk than in NCs.

Next, we compared the electron‐phonon interaction in the nano‐ and bulk samples. From the temperature‐induced broadening of the Te4+ emission under excitation of 395 nm, the electron‐phonon coupling and the phonon energy have been derived using the following equation[

42

]:

where FWHM (T) is the full width at half maximum of the PL band, S is the Huang‐Rhys factor, which describes the strength of electron‐phonon interactions, ћω ph is the frequency of the phonon, and k B is the Boltzmann constant (≈8.62 × 10−2 meV K−1). By fitting the experimental data to Equation (1), ћωph is determined as 23.6 and 22.9 meV and S as 22.8 and 24.0 for Cs2ZrCl6:Te4+ NCs and bulk powders, respectively (Figure S10, Supporting Information). Such similar S values for both nano‐ and bulk crystals are consistent with previous reports and indicate the presence of strong electron‐phonon coupling (S > 10) in the soft halide matrix for both kinds of samples.15 Thus, this observation additionally highlights the similarity of the vibronic structure in Cs2ZrCl6:Te4+ NCs and bulk powders. Moreover, from the measured temperature‐dependent FWHM of the Te4+ PL band, the energetic disorder, Γ 0 (related to inhomogeneous broadening), the exciton‐optical phonon coupling strength, ΓLO , and the optical phonon energy, 𝜂𝜔LO , have been calculated using the Fröhlich longitudinal optical phonon broadening model:[
43 , 44 , 45
]

The term Γ 0 could be affected by the size and shape of the materials.[
43 , 44
] However, it constitutes almost the same value of ≈266 meV for both Cs2ZrCl6:Te4+ NCs and bulk powders (Figure S11, Supporting Information). Similarly, all other calculated parameters have similar values for both samples: calculated ΓLO and 𝜂𝜔LO are 150 meV and 18.5 meV, respectively. Comparing the results of the two models, one can notice that Equations (1) and (2) provide very close values of the phonon energies, which additionally supports the assumption that the contribution of acoustic phonons over the fitted temperature range is negligible.[
46
] This demonstrates that the soft halide lattice of the host material significantly influences the optical properties of both Cs2ZrCl6:Te4+ NCs and bulk powders; however, no significant differences in the electron‐phonon coupling for the NCs and the bulk are observed. These results suggest that the exciton‐phonon coupling is not the reason for the reduced PL QY of Te4+ emission in NCs.

We have then considered the trends in the PL peak positions of the Cs2ZrCl6 and Te4+ related PL bands at different temperatures. In Figure 2d (upper panel), one can see that for the bulk powder, the peak of the host emission remains unchanged with increasing temperature, in contrast to the noticeable blue shift for NCs. Such behavior has been reported for other lead‐free perovskites and has been attributed to the expansion of their lattice upon heating.[
47
] Interestingly, the opposite tendency is observed for the Te4+ band (Figure 2d, lower panel). While for the bulk powder, the position of this band almost linearly shifts with increasing temperature, for NCs it starts to be sensitive to heating only above 150 K. Therefore, we assume that in bulk powders the change of the cell volume leads to the distortion of the TeCl6 2− octahedra while for NCs the compensation is realized via displacements in the soft host lattice.

Then, the temperature dependence of the average PL lifetimes at 450 nm (emission from a host) and 580 nm (emission from Te dopant) for both Cs2ZrCl6:Te4+ NCs and bulk powders was investigated (Figure 2e). The excitation wavelength for PL decay measurements was 250 nm. As illustrated in Figure 2f, the emission of Cs2ZrCl6 host is hypothesized to be thermally activated delayed fluorescence (TADF) which is caused by the reverse intersystem crossing (RISC) of excitons from the singlet to triplet states and back[

48

] In such a system, the triplet state is usually not emissive; however, the carriers with enough thermal energy can return to the “bright” singlet energy level with subsequent radiative recombination. Furthermore, the previous report suggested that in Cs2ZrCl6 the excited states are STEs, and radiative recombination is allowed for both singlet and triplet STEs.[

22

] Based on this conclusion, we describe the host emission according to a TADF model that includes two STE states: one triplet and one singlet. Therefore, for NCs, the PL decay of the host could be well fitted with a biexponential function where one component can be attributed to the delayed emission from the singlet STE and another one to the triplet STE emission.[

49

] Interestingly, one more fast decay exponent is observed in bulk powders at lower temperatures (T <150K, see Table S1, Supporting Information) with a characteristic time of ≈8 µs. The existence of this fast decay component decreases the average value of the PL lifetime for bulk crystals at lower temperatures. At higher temperatures (>150K) the average PL decay times show no significant difference between NCs and bulk crystals (Table S1, Supporting Information). The calculated average PL lifetimes were used to estimate the energy difference between the singlet and triplet states, Δ, by the model previously suggested for TADF systems[

22

]:

where τt and τs are the lifetimes for the triplet and singlet states of Cs2ZrCl6 host, and kb is the Boltzmann constant. The Δ values are calculated to be 0.063 and 0.054 eV for NCs and bulk powders, respectively (Figure S12, Supporting Information), which agrees well with previous reports.[
50
] Such Δ values indicate that the triplet state of a host in Cs2ZrCl6:Te4+ NCs lies lower than that in the bulk crystals, which can be explained as the influence of the weak confinement regime: the decrease of size leads to reduced energy of the STEs (the decline of triplet‐state of the host in Figure 2f). A similar effect has been previously reported for Cs3Cu2X5 (X = Cl, Br, I) perovskite NCs.[
51
]

As has been stated before, the PL band at 580 nm originates from the electronic transitions in Te4+. As reported elsewhere,[
52
] Te4+ has two states that can be excited in the UV–vis range: singlet 1P1 and triplet 3P n (n = 0, 1, 2). The energy of the 1P1 – 1S0 transition is ≈4.35 eV, and it is higher than the calculated band gap value for Cs2ZrCl6 (3.71 eV according to ref. [38]). Therefore, the singlet Te4+ state lies in the conduction band of the host, and the observed emission at 580 nm is caused by the transition from the triplet state 3P1,0 to 1S0. Note that 3P0 → 1S0 transition is dipole allowed, while the 3P2 → 1S0 transition is forbidden by selection rules. It is still under debate whether one or both triplet states participate in radiative relaxation[
50 , 53
]; therefore, here onward we will refer to this energy level as 3P1,0. Temperature‐dependent studies of time‐resolved PL allow us to analyze the energy transfer from host STEs to the 3P1,0 state. For the bulk crystals, radiative recombination dynamics from Te4+ can be well fitted with a single exponent in the whole temperature range, indicating the presence of only one emitting pathway (Figure S13d, Supporting Information). At T< 130 K, there are no observable differences in the PL lifetimes of Cs2ZrCl6:Te4+ NCs and bulk powders (Figure S13b,d, Supporting Information). For Cs2ZrCl6:Te4+ NCs at lower temperatures (50–130 K), the PL at 580 nm decays monoexponentially, and the characteristic decay time (τ1) changes from ≈10 to 4 µs (Figure S13b, Supporting Information). Upon further heating, the PL lifetime of this component (τ1) does not change significantly, but its contribution to the average PL decay decreases (Table S2, Supporting Information). We have noticed that the amplitude of τ1 replicates the temperature‐dependent trend of the radiative rate of both NCs and bulk powders (Figure S14b, Supporting Information), as well as the PL integral intensity at 580 nm of NCs (Figure S14a, Supporting Information). Therefore, this decay component can be assigned to the radiative recombination from the 3P1,0 state. However, at T >130 K, an additional exponent (τ2) with a longer lifetime should be added to the fitting (Figure S15, Supporting Information). The amplitude of this long‐lived component linearly grows with temperature, and therefore the average PL lifetime increases (Figure 2e). We suppose that below host energy levels there is an intermediate defect state which might originate from the insufficient ligand passivation in Cs2ZrCl6:Te4+ NCs. When the temperature is low, traps are frozen and the charge directly transfers from STEs of Cs2ZrCl6 to the dopant 3P1,0 level. As heating continues, thermal energy surpasses the activation energy of defects, resulting in more effective trapping of charge carriers. At this point, there are two possible options for further relaxation: i) non‐radiatively by heat dissipation; and ii) radiatively after detrapping and further transfer to the 3P1,0 level of Te4+ dopant. The last relaxation path results in the appearance of the additional component in the PL decay and overall prolongation of the lifetimes.

To further understand why Cs2ZrCl6:Te4+ bulk powders experience less thermal quenching during heating in comparison with NCs, we have used Equation (4) to analyze the integrated emission intensity:[

54

]

where I 0 is the integrated emission intensity at 0 K, A is a constant, and Ea is the activation energy of the nonradiative channels. The activation energies for Cs2ZrCl6:Te4+ NCs and bulk powders are determined from the analysis of the Arrhenius plot of the PL intensity at 580 nm (Figure S16, Supporting Information). The activation energy for NCs is only 66 meV, while for the bulk it is almost 4 times higher (206 meV). Such a drastic difference can be explained by the larger lattice displacements in Cs2ZrCl6:Te4+ NCs due to the lower chemical stoichiometry of their surface and ligand‐induced traps. It also results in the already mentioned larger Jahn‐Teller splitting, a more pronounced distortion of the 3P1,0 emitting state, and therefore in a decrease in the activation energy.[
55
] In continuation of this reasoning, the increase in size (and hence lowering surface to volume ratio) and a more balanced elemental ratio toward stoichiometric perovskite (2:1:6) would be beneficial for enhancing PL intensity from dopants.

Furthermore, we synthesized Cs2ZrCl6:Te4+ NCs co‐doped with lanthanide ions to demonstrate that sensitization of Ln3+ through Te4+ triplet states is allowed in NCs, and results in the appearance of emission in the NIR range. Such an energy transfer process has been demonstrated in bulk crystals previously.[
56 , 57
] Here, we prepared Yb3+, Er3+, and Nd3+ doped Cs2ZrCl6:Te4+ NCs by using the chloride salt of each lanthanide ion that can be easily dissolved in mesitylene. The nominal and actual doping amounts of these three Ln3+ cations measured by the inductively coupled plasma optical emission spectrometry (ICP‐OES) are summarized in Tables S4–S6 (Supporting Information), respectively. It has been previously reported that in lead halide perovskites, the effective energy transfer to Yb3+ or Er3+ ions requires an intermediate defect state that has energy at least twice the value of the emissive transition in the lanthanide ion.[
58
] To examine the role of the Te4+ triplet state as the intermediate level for the lanthanide ions, we excited the co‐doped Cs2ZrCl6:Te4+Ln3+ NCs with 395 nm light (in the S0 →3P1 transition of Te4+) and measured the PL spectra in the NIR region.

As shown in Figure

4a–c, upon excitation at 395 nm, typical sharp emission peaks that result from the 2F5/2→2F7/2 transition of Yb3+, the 4I13/2→4I15/2 transition of Er3+ at 1539 nm, 4F3/2→4IJ transitions of Nd3+ at 1073 (4I11/2) and 1357 (4I13/2) nm are observed. Energy dispersive spectroscopy (EDS) mapping shows the uniform distribution of the Ln3+ ions across the co‐doped Cs2ZrCl6:Te4+ Ln3+ NCs (Figures S19, Supporting Information). Expectedly, an increase in lanthanide doping leads to PL quenching.[

59

] The maximum PL intensity is observed when the concentrations of Yb3+, Er3+, and Nd3+ are 1.9, 1.9, and 1.5 mol%, respectively. The state‐of‐the‐art PL QY of Cs2ZrCl6: Er3+ bulk powders reached up to 6.1% in the NIR region,[

56

] while to the best of our knowledge, there were no reports on PL QYs for the Cs2ZrCl6:Te4+Ln3+ NCs yet. We have estimated PL QYs for the Cs2ZrCl6:Te4+ Ln3+ NCs by relative measurements (see Experimental section for details); the respective values for Cs2ZrCl6:Te4+ Ln3+ NCs with 1.9 mol% of Yb3+, 1.9 mol% of Er3+, and 1.5 mol% of Nd3+ are 0.2, 0.3, and 0.4%. The corresponding average PL lifetimes of the most efficient Yb3+, Er3+, and Nd3+ doped NCs reach 5.2, 4.5, and 4.5 ms. PL decay curves and the detailed fitting parameters are shown in Figure 4d–f and Table S7 (Supporting Information), respectively.
